// Clustered Index (data sorted physically)
Table Storage: [1-Alice][2-Bob][3-Carol][4-Dave] ← sorted by ID
Query: SELECT * WHERE id = 3 → directly at position 3

// Non-Clustered Index (separate structure)
Index: { 'Alice'→row1, 'Bob'→row2, 'Carol'→row3 }
Query: SELECT * WHERE name = 'Carol'
   → look up 'Carol' in index → get row pointer → fetch row

// Rule of thumb:
// Clustered: 1 per table, for primary key / range queries
// Non-clustered: many per table, for frequent lookup columns

// DEADLOCK in DBMS
T1: LOCK(A) → waiting for B
T2: LOCK(B) → waiting for A
→ Neither can proceed = DEADLOCK

// Detection: Wait-for graph — if cycle exists → deadlock
// Resolution: Kill one transaction (victim), rollback

// LIVELOCK
T1: Releases lock for T2 → T2 releases for T1 → T1 releases...
// Both keep yielding, neither makes progress
// Fix: Random backoff or priority-based scheduling

// Prevention Protocols:
// Wait-Die: older waits, younger dies (non-preemptive)
// Wound-Wait: older wounds (preempts) younger, younger waits

// Thrashing Scenario
RAM = 1 GB, Total demand by all processes = 4 GB

Loop:
    Process P1 needs page → PAGE FAULT → swap in from disk
    Process P2 needs page → PAGE FAULT → evict P1's page
    Process P1 needs page again → PAGE FAULT → evict P2's page
    ...(repeats endlessly)
→ CPU utilization drops to near 0% — all time spent on I/O

// Fix:
// Working Set Model: give each process only its 'working set' of pages
// PFF (Page Fault Frequency): if rate too high → add frames to process

// INTERNAL FRAGMENTATION (Paging)
Page size = 4KB
Process needs 6KB → allocated 2 pages = 8KB
Wasted inside = 8KB - 6KB = 2KB ← internal fragmentation

// EXTERNAL FRAGMENTATION (Contiguous allocation)
Memory: [FREE:10KB][USED][FREE:10KB][USED][FREE:10KB]
Total free = 30KB, but largest contiguous = 10KB
Process needs 25KB → CANNOT allocate! ← external fragmentation
// Fix: Compaction (move processes to merge free space)

// NON-PREEMPTIVE (FCFS)
P1(burst=24) starts → runs to completion → P2 starts
// P2 waits 24ms regardless of its own burst time → convoy effect

// PREEMPTIVE (Round Robin, quantum=4)
P1(24ms) → runs 4ms → P2(3ms) → runs 3ms (done) → P3(3ms) → P1(4ms)...
// CPU shared fairly — better response time

// PREEMPTIVE Priority
P_low running → P_high arrives → CPU TAKEN from P_low → P_high runs
// Risk: Starvation of low priority processes
// Fix: Aging (gradually increase priority of waiting processes)

// Process State Diagram

NEW → admitted → READY
READY → scheduler dispatch → RUNNING
RUNNING → interrupt / time quantum expired → READY
RUNNING → I/O or event wait → WAITING
WAITING → I/O or event complete → READY
RUNNING → exit → TERMINATED

// PCB (Process Control Block) stores:
// Process ID, State, Program Counter, CPU registers,
// Memory limits, Open file list, Scheduling info

// NAT in action (PAT — Port Address Translation)

Private Network:          Internet:
192.168.1.10:5000  ──→  Router  ──→  203.0.113.1:10001 → Web Server
192.168.1.11:5000  ──→  Router  ──→  203.0.113.1:10002 → Web Server
192.168.1.12:7000  ──→  Router  ──→  203.0.113.1:10003 → Web Server

// NAT Table in Router:
// Private IP:Port        →  Public IP:Port
// 192.168.1.10:5000     →  203.0.113.1:10001
// 192.168.1.11:5000     →  203.0.113.1:10002
// 192.168.1.12:7000     →  203.0.113.1:10003

// Reply from web server comes to 203.0.113.1:10001
// Router translates back → forwards to 192.168.1.10:5000

// HUB (Layer 1 – Physical)
Device A sends to Device C
Hub broadcasts to ALL ports: A, B, C, D
// B and D receive unwanted data → collision domain

// SWITCH (Layer 2 – Data Link)
MAC Table: { C → Port 3, D → Port 4 }
Device A sends to C (MAC: AA:CC)
Switch → looks up MAC table → sends ONLY to Port 3
// Efficient, no unnecessary broadcasts (for unicast)

// ROUTER (Layer 3 – Network)
Packet from 192.168.1.10 → 8.8.8.8
Router checks routing table:
  8.0.0.0/8 → via ISP gateway (203.0.113.1)
Router forwards packet to ISP gateway

// Vertical Scaling
Server: 8 CPU, 16 GB RAM
   ↓ upgrade
Server: 32 CPU, 128 GB RAM
// Pros: No code change
// Cons: Expensive, hardware limit, single point of failure

// Horizontal Scaling
Server_1: 8 CPU, 16 GB RAM
   + add servers
Server_1, Server_2, Server_3 ← Load Balancer distributes traffic
// Pros: Cost-effective, fault-tolerant, infinite scale
// Cons: Need stateless services or shared session store (Redis)

// Without CDN
User in Mumbai → request → Server in US → response (high latency ~200ms)

// With CDN
User in Mumbai → request → CDN Edge (Mumbai) → response (~10ms)

// CDN Cache Miss Flow
User → CDN Edge (Mumbai) → cache miss → Origin Server (US)
Origin → CDN Edge stores copy + sends to user
Next user → CDN Edge (Mumbai) → cache HIT → fast response

// What CDNs cache: images, videos, CSS, JS, HTML, fonts
// What NOT to cache: user-specific data, real-time API responses

// KAFKA — Event Streaming
Producer (Order Service) → Kafka Topic 'orders' → stored durably
    ↓ Consumer Group 1: Inventory Service reads events
    ↓ Consumer Group 2: Analytics Service reads SAME events
    ↓ Consumer Group 3: Notification Service reads SAME events
// Multiple consumers read independently; events retained for days
// Great for: event sourcing, stream processing

// RABBITMQ — Task Queue
Producer → Exchange → Queue → Consumer_1 (processes task, deleted)
                     Queue → Consumer_2 (gets next task)
// Each message consumed by ONE consumer
// Great for: job queues, email sending, payment processing

// Decision Guide:
// Need replay / multiple consumers of same event? → Kafka
// Need guaranteed single delivery, complex routing? → RabbitMQ

// S – Single Responsibility
BAD:  class UserManager { login(), sendEmail(), generateReport() }
GOOD: class UserAuth { login() }
      class EmailService { sendEmail() }
      class ReportGenerator { generate() }

// O – Open/Closed
BAD:  if shape=='circle': area=PI*r*r
      elif shape=='rect': area=l*w  // must modify to add new shape
GOOD: class Circle { area() } class Rect { area() }  // extend, don't modify

// L – Liskov Substitution
BAD:  class Square extends Rectangle
      Square.setWidth(5) → also sets height → breaks Rectangle contract
GOOD: Both implement Shape interface separately

// D – Dependency Inversion
BAD:  class Service { db = new MySQLDB() }  // tightly coupled
GOOD: class Service { def __init__(db: DatabaseInterface): self.db = db }
